.TH OBJDUMP09 1 2026-11-10 "bcc09 0.12.0" "bcc09 Manual"
.SH NAME
objdump09, nm09, size09 \- show what is in 6809 object files, libraries
and executables
.SH SYNOPSIS
.B objdump09
.RB [ \-n | \-s ]
.RB [ \-o ]
.IR file ...
.br
.B nm09
.RB [ \-o ]
.IR file ...
.br
.B size09
.IR file ...
.SH DESCRIPTION
.B objdump09
reads object files from
.BR as09 (1),
libraries from
.BR ar09 (1)
(each member in turn), and executables from
.BR ld09 (1).
It is dev86's objdump86; called
.B nm09
it lists symbols, and called
.B size09
sizes, as
.B \-n
and
.B \-s
make it do.
.PP
By default it shows everything: an object file's module name, its
symbols, its segments' sizes and its code, with the relocations; an
executable's header (entry point, flags), sizes and symbols.
.SH OPTIONS
.TP
.B \-n
List the symbols, as
.BR nm (1)
does: each one's value, a letter for its kind, and its name.
The letter is
.B T
or
.B t
for code (segments 0 and 1),
.B D
or
.B d
for data,
.B a
for an absolute value,
.B b
for a common block that is local,
.B C
for a common block, and
.B U
for a symbol that is undefined (imported); upper case is exported.
.TP
.B \-s
Show the sizes of the code (text), data and BSS, as
.BR size (1)
does, with a total line for several files.
.TP
.B \-o
With
.BR \-n ,
put the file's name before each symbol.
.SH EXIT STATUS
0, or 1 if no file is named.
.SH SEE ALSO
.BR bcc09 (1),
.BR as09 (1),
.BR ld09 (1),
.BR ar09 (1),
.BR objcopy09 (1)
